  "schema": "fdp-mini-manual-expected-v1",
  "about": [
    "What tools/init must read out of the two committed mini-manual PDFs",
    "(docs/plan/init.md §8 and §15.1). Hand-authored from mini-manual.html and",
    "verified against pdfplumber 0.11.10 with the settings of §8.4.",
    "",
    "`variants` holds what depends on the layout: the page count, the heading",
    "tree, the page furniture that §8.2 drops, the tables §8.4 finds and how",
    "many chunks of each kind §10 cuts. `catalog` is outside `variants`: both",
    "variants print the same causes, alarms and signals, and §9.1 is expected to",
    "return the same catalog from either PDF, which is the property INI-06",
    "tests.",
    "",
    "Field notes:",
    "  headings.title  the text after the section number, exactly as printed;",
    "                  the 8.2.k headings of the clean variant carry the",
    "                  condition id after the title, and the realistic variant",
    "                  prints no 8.2.k heading at all — the same three",
    "                  conditions open a group row inside the spanning table.",
    "  furniture       the raw margin lines §8.2 drops, in page order.",
    "  tables.rows     data rows, excluding the repeated header row and, for a",
    "                  troubleshooting table, excluding the group rows; the",
    "                  fragment of a row a page break cut counts once, with its",
    "                  tail folded into its head (§8.4 row continuation).",
    "  tables.merged_from  how many per-page fragments §8.4 merges into the",
    "                  table; 1 when the table sits on one page.",
    "  subsystem       the CON enum value; the PDF prints it with spaces for",
    "                  underscores, the way the real manual does (R-09).",
    "  signal_moves    MAN's vocabulary verbatim (R-10): the direction word the",
    "                  sentence uses, and `note` carrying the recognised onset",
    "                  and phase, onset first, `any` for \"in every state\".",
    "                  `load_cycle_rate` is a behaviour, not a signal tag."
  ],
